Slide 2 — Problem Setup
	•	Learned planners (PLUTO, PDM, GameFormer) minimise average trajectory error across all scenario types
	•	Implicit assumption: one model with enough capacity handles all behaviours
	•	In reality:
	•	Different scenarios require fundamentally different behaviours (e.g. stopping vs continuous motion)
	•	A single model must represent conflicting behaviours
	•	WTA training often collapses to a dominant mode in practice

Core question:

What if the planner knew the scene type and activated a specialist?

⸻

Slide 3 — Why nuPlan Makes This Non-Trivial
	•	Offline metrics (minADE / minFDE):
	•	Averaged over recorded frames
	•	Evaluate imitation
	•	Closed-loop simulation:
	•	Reactive agents
	•	Traffic rule penalties
	•	Progress tracking
	•	Evaluate decision-making
	•	Example:
	•	A planner that never stops:
	•	Good offline score
	•	Fails in simulation
	•	The evaluation gap is structural:
	•	Offline metrics do not capture stop behaviour, yield compliance, or reactive response
	•	Key insight: Offline metrics ≠ driving quality
	•	Design target: optimise for simulation, not regression

⸻

Slide 4 — Scene Classification
	•	6 scene classes (map topology + ego intent):
	•	Left turn (intersection)
	•	Straight (intersection)
	•	Right turn (intersection)
	•	Straight (non-intersection)
	•	U-turn
	•	Complex / other
	•	Dataset:
	•	~800K scenarios
	•	Long-tailed distribution

Key issue:

Class imbalance → router ignores rare but critical behaviours

⸻

Slide 5 — EMoE Architecture

Built on PLUTO with 3 additions:
	•	SceneRouter
	•	Predicts scene class
	•	Inverse-frequency weighted loss
	•	SceneModeQueryGenerator (Hard)
	•	Routes each sample to exactly one expert (R = 1)
	•	EMoEPlanningDecoder
	•	Expert-specific decoding
	•	Ka = 24 anchors
	•	FOPE positional encoding
	•	score_attn → pi_head → output

Design choice — Hard routing (R = 1):
	•	One expert accountable per scene
	•	Avoids gradient interference between experts
	•	Interpretable at inference

Key idea:

Each expert learns a restricted behaviour space → reduces interference

⸻

Slide 6 — What is an Anchor?
	•	Each anchor = a learnable trajectory template representing one possible future
	•	Ka = 24 anchors collectively cover behaviour space:
	•	Forward motion at multiple speeds
	•	Turning arcs
	•	Deceleration to stop
	•	Decoder selects and refines the best-matching anchor per sample

Key insight:

If anchors don’t include a behaviour, the model cannot learn it

⸻

Slide 7 — Trajectory Anchors & Mode Collapse
	•	Ka = 24 learnable templates
	•	WTA updates only the best-matching anchor per sample

Problem:
	•	Zero initialisation → identical anchors
	•	WTA reinforces one mode
	•	Others receive no gradients

Observed:
	•	0/24 anchors produced stopping trajectories (T1–T5)
	•	Measured via velocity threshold across predictions

Impact:
	•	No stopping → red light violations, failure to yield
	•	Severe penalties in simulation
	•	Offline ADE/FDE underweight this failure — dominated by moving frames

Key insight:

Mode collapse is structural, not just optimisation noise

⸻

Slide 8 — Fix: Geometric Anchor Initialisation (T6)
	•	Anchors initialised with geometric diversity:
	•	Multiple directions
	•	Multiple speed profiles
	•	Explicit stopping modes
	•	Anchors frozen after initialisation:
	•	Prevents collapse of anchor geometry under WTA
	•	Decoder adapts around fixed anchors

Results:
	•	17/24 anchors produce stopping trajectories (epoch 2)
	•	Previously: 0/24
	•	Offline metrics decrease (expected)
	•	Simulation score increases (projected 0.86–0.89)

Conclusion:

Anchor design directly controls reachable behaviour

⸻

Slide 9 — Router: Class Imbalance
	•	Naive training:
	•	Predicts majority class
	•	~76% accuracy → uninformative

Fixes:
	•	Inverse-frequency class weights
	•	WeightedRandomSampler
	•	Label smoothing (ε = 0.1)

Result:
	•	Router accuracy → 0.94

Key insight:

Without balanced routing, experts cannot specialise

⸻

Slide 10 — Loss Function

Loss	Weight	Purpose
L_reg	1.0	Trajectory regression (xy, WTA)
L_cls	2.0	Mode selection
L_col	0.5	Collision penalty (ESDF)
L_pred	1.0	Agent prediction
L_router	1.5	Scene classification

	•	Additional:
	•	Yaw smoothness loss (×0.15)
	•	Weights tuned empirically across runs
	•	Early bug:
	•	WTA selection used all channels instead of xy-only
	•	Fix reduced minFDE from 7.16 → 1.10

Key idea:

Each loss targets a specific failure mode

⸻

Slide 11 — ESDF Collision Loss
	•	nuPlan cost maps:
	•	Signed distance fields (ESDF)

Pipeline:
	•	Predicted trajectory → grid coordinates
	•	Sample ESDF via grid_sample
	•	Negative values → collision penalty

Advantages:
	•	Fully differentiable
	•	No explicit geometry checks

Key insight:

Provides direct gradient signal from collision → shapes trajectory

⸻

Slide 12 — Auxiliary Supervision
	•	Applied to decoded queries (not final output):
	•	Comfort proxy
	•	Penalises jerk
	•	Progress signal
	•	Encourages forward motion
	•	Inspired by Hydra-style supervision

Role:
	•	Improves smoothness
	•	Stabilises training

